= Defining innovations
innovations are changes - these can be…
- technical
- social
- organizational
- scientific

Innovations can include
- New combinations
- New organizational structures
- New methods
- New contents or qualities
- New markets

= Social innovations
Developing and deploying effective solutions to challenging social and environmental issues — not limited to any one sector, but requiring collaboration across government, business, and nonprofits.

This includes
- Social justice
- Equality
- overall well-being.
- Education
- Healthcare
- Human rights

= Three big NO's in (social) innovation
- Proven Faliure
  - some interventions have no impact at all
  - Often because the actual customer/user/usage case has not been studied carefully
- Negative social outcomes
  - Good intentions can still cause severe harm — immediately or downstream.
- Unethical behavior
  - Kant's golden rule
  - Rawls “veil of ignorance”
  - Utilitarianism
  - Ethical principles of benevolence, non-maleficience & fairness

= The social impact compass

#figure(
  image("assets/image-56.png"),
  caption: []
)

- Value to society
  - How does the intended outcome deliver societal value?
- Efficacy
  - How certain are we of the effects of the solution?
- Impact magnitude
  - How complete is the solution for each beneficiary?
- Scalability
  - How much of the affected community can this model address?
- Mission alignment
  - To what extent is the organization outcome oriented?
- Enviromental and social governance
  - How responsible is the intervention with regard to environmental and social governance?

= Unsuccessful
Even unsuccessful innovations have contributed
substantially to what came after them
- Some of them were sold to competitors, some failed to keep innovating, some were caught by law

= Diffusion of innovations
Innovation =
- A (new) idea or practice,
- a (new) product,
- a (new) technique or technology

== Acceptance of innovations
One broad and popular framework:
#figure(
  image("assets/image-58.png"),
  caption: [Technology acceptance model by Fred Davis, Rooted in the theory of planned behaviour (TPB) by *Ajzen & Fishbein*]
)
Acceptance as a result of$dots$
- Expectations regarding the performance of a technology (is it doing what it is supposed to?)
- Expectations regarding the (necessary) effort (what do I have to do)?
- Plus: social influence factors (Should I do that? Does everyone do it?)
- New: Further facilitating factors (e.g., trust)

== The role of trust
- Trust = a risky action, a trustee (a subject/person) is willing to risk an object, person, or process that he or she cannot control
- With full control, trust becomes unnecessary
- Central question: Whom and what can I trust?
- Competence, benevolence, and reputation → trustworthiness indicators (AI507 - AI and society/Lectures/3 - Social Cognition)

== Adaption is also a cognitive process
Basic idea: Technology = cognitive construct that requires encoding, storage and retrieval

If technologies are perceived to be similar to familiar or compatible with
existing technologies, or related to positive concepts (my family &
friends), they are more likely to be perceived as being useful